#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192256 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc1fbb99-83e1-52ba-83f1-367de07351a6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Improper Integral Using a Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "522a785c-3e1e-5515-82c9-8980b448bee4" :question/math-academy-id "e-10553" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_1^\\infty x^3\\cdot 2^{-x^4} \\,\\textrm{d}x$." :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=x^4$. Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= 4x^3 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 14 \\textrm{d}u =x^3\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty$ when $x\\to \\infty$, the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x^4$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{1}^{∞}x^{3} \\cdot 2^{-x^{4}}dx &= \\frac{1}{4}∫_{1}^{∞}2^{-u}du\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a$, integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{1}^{∞}x^{3} \\cdot 2^{-x^{4}}dx &= \\frac{1}{4}∫_{1}^{∞}2^{-u}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot lim_(a → ∞)∫_{1}^{a}2^{-u}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot lim_(a → ∞)[- \\frac{2^{-u}}{\\ln 2}]_{1}^{a} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{4\\ln 2} \\cdot lim_(a → ∞)[2^{-u}]_{1}^{a} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{4\\ln 2} \\cdot (lim_(a → ∞)\\frac{1}{2^{a}} - \\frac{1}{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{2^x}\n$$\n\nshown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{2^{x}} = \\infty$\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{1}{2^{x}} = 0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4004/Source/Images/769170756b4db689d820f57e3f8d9791.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{1}^{∞}x^{3} \\cdot 2^{-x^{4}}dx &= -\\frac{1}{4\\ln 2} \\cdot (lim_(a → ∞)\\frac{1}{2^{a}} - \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{4\\ln 2} \\cdot (0 - \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8\\ln 2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192257 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f0d268f-97f9-5db8-863d-dfc237afa546" :knowledge-point/title "Improper Integral Involving the Exponential Function"}] :question/id #uuid "6ad02a19-7b07-5e07-8388-b46fd1cc48ae" :question/math-academy-id "e-10554" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^\\infty e^{-3x} \\,\\textrm{d}x$." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int e^{px + q}\\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac1p e^{px+q} + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a$, integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{-1}^{∞}e^{-3x}dx &= lim_(a → ∞)∫_{-1}^{a}e^{-3x}dx \\\\\n&= lim_(a → ∞)[- \\frac{e^{-3x}}{3}]_{-1}^{a} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot lim_(a → ∞)[e^{-3x}]_{-1}^{a} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot (lim_(a → ∞)e^{-3a} - e^{3})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=e^{-3x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} e^{-3x} = \\infty$\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} e^{-3x} =0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4004/Source/Images/121e8b397fd3d1ba5e806dfe5ca38245.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{-1}^{∞}e^{-3x} &= -\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot (lim_(a → ∞)e^{-3a} - e^{3}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot (0 - e^{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{e^{3}}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192292 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e84d5ab0-cec4-5988-9db3-8e296590d279" :knowledge-point/title "Improper Integral Involving Other Exponential Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "37af544e-feab-52b0-bcef-887c723093b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-10764" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{0} 3\\cdot 2^{2x+1} \\,\\textrm{d}x$." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int b^{\\,px + q}\\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac1{p\\ln(b)} b^{\\,px+q} + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a$, integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to-\\infty$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{- ∞}^{0}3 \\cdot 2^{2x + 1}dx &= 3 \\cdot lim_(a → - ∞)∫_{a}^{0}2^{2x + 1}dx \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot lim_(a → - ∞)[(2^{2x + 1})/(2\\ln (2))]_{a}^{0} \\\\\n&= (3 \\cdot 2^{1})/(2\\ln (2)) \\cdot lim_(a → - ∞)[2^{2x}]_{a}^{0} \\\\\n&= (3)/(\\ln (2)) \\cdot lim_(a → - ∞)(2^{0} - 2^{2a}) \\\\\n&= (3)/(\\ln (2)) \\cdot (1 - lim_(a → - ∞)2^{2a})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=2^{2x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} 2^{2x} = 0$\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} 2^{2x} = \\infty$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4004/Source/Images/e9da045e68ed322bd54138b5cd02b995.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{- ∞}^{0}3 \\cdot 2^{2x + 1}dx &= (3)/(\\ln (2)) \\cdot (1 - lim_(a → - ∞)2^{2a}) \\\\\n&= (3)/(\\ln (2)) \\cdot (1 - 0) \\\\\n&= (3)/(\\ln (2))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193070 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc1fbb99-83e1-52ba-83f1-367de07351a6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Improper Integral Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b04158bd-8b5c-53e3-b00e-0e5e20eda3a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191192} {:answer/id #uuid "bd4e7957-ae3a-58e4-a82a-6d62e142f275" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191193} {:answer/id #uuid "04ba4369-c485-555e-ae2c-de879c990dc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191194} {:answer/id #uuid "211cfcf9-6419-5a27-ba51-5cedc1f607ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191195} {:answer/id #uuid "70316794-503a-5bfc-ac8a-8f094446e8d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191196}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd4e7957-ae3a-58e4-a82a-6d62e142f275" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191193} :answer-field/id #uuid "3143fafd-7355-519f-8915-bb5002dd4cd4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191191}] :question/id #uuid "826720bb-562f-595c-b16e-c49b0073360a" :question/math-academy-id "q-20891" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $∫_{0}^{∞}\\frac{e^{-\\sqrt{2x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}dx$."}]}
